WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.583
If you sew one eye shut, what happens to the cortical cells it used to drive?

2
00:00:08.617 --> 00:00:14.417
In 1963, Wiesel and Hubel reported what happens when a kitten grows up with one eye

3
00:00:14.417 --> 00:00:15.417
closed.

4
00:00:15.817 --> 00:00:18.557
They sutured the lids of one eye at or near birth.

5
00:00:19.417 --> 00:00:21.697
They recorded after about three months of closure.

6
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:26.307
Very few cortical cells could be driven by the closed eye.

7
00:00:27.267 --> 00:00:29.107
Yet its retina still responded.

8
00:00:30.047 --> 00:00:33.707
So did its LGN cells, though those serving the closed eye looked smaller.

9
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:36.327
So the eye was not broken.

10
00:00:37.187 --> 00:00:38.407
What changed was in the brain.

11
00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.150
An adult cat with one eye closed for months showed no such change.

12
00:00:44.083 --> 00:00:46.223
In 1970 they asked when.

13
00:00:47.083 --> 00:00:50.983
They closed one eye in kittens of different ages, for different lengths of time.

14
00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:57.003
Predict. That closure from birth lasted months, so it spanned many ages.

15
00:00:58.163 --> 00:01:00.243
Compare brief closures begun at different ages.

16
00:01:01.123 --> 00:01:03.863
Is there one switch-off point, or a rise and a fall?

17
00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:11.547
A rise and a fall.

18
00:01:12.487 --> 00:01:15.967
In kittens, susceptibility began around the start of the fourth week.

19
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:20.057
It stayed high until somewhere between six and eight weeks.

20
00:01:20.857 --> 00:01:22.517
Then it declined through the third month.

21
00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:28.287
So a closure from birth lasting 3 months overlaps those weeks, which fits its large

22
00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:29.287
effect.

23
00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:34.890
At the peak, the effect was fast.

24
00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:40.170
Some kittens had the right eye closed for only 6 days, in the fourth or fifth week.

25
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:45.470
Recording in the left hemisphere, the closed right eye is the group 1 side.

26
00:01:46.483 --> 00:01:49.883
The number of cells it could drive fell to a fraction of normal.

27
00:01:50.783 --> 00:01:53.943
Most of the histogram moved toward group 7, the open eye.

28
00:01:54.917 --> 00:01:57.357
Six days of unequal vision had been enough.

29
00:01:58.317 --> 00:01:59.757
The anatomy follows.

30
00:02:00.737 --> 00:02:04.597
Hubel, Wiesel and Simon LeVay closed one eye in infant macaque monkeys.

31
00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:07.950
Later they traced the stripes in layer 4C.

32
00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:12.170
The open eye's stripes had widened, and the closed eye's had narrowed.

33
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.100
In kittens, single thalamic axons serving a deprived eye were later seen to lose

34
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.440
branches within about a week.

35
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:26.610
So a closure in this window changes the territory each eye's axons hold, not only

36
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:27.610
how cells respond.

37
00:02:28.683 --> 00:02:32.863
Takeaway: in the young, a few days of unequal vision move the balance.

38
00:02:32.863 --> 00:02:35.283
In the adult, months do not.

39
00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:37.347
That leaves a question.

40
00:02:38.287 --> 00:02:42.487
Did the closed eye lose because it was unused, or because the other eye was not?
